-- title:
--   Lévy's upward theorem
-- statement:
--   **Lévy's upward theorem.** Let $(\mathcal F_n)$ be a filtration on a finite measure space $(\Omega,\mu)$, and let $\mathcal F_\infty=\bigvee_n\mathcal F_n$. For every real function $g$,
--   $$\mathbb E[g\mid\mathcal F_n]\longrightarrow\mathbb E[g\mid\mathcal F_\infty]\quad\text{almost everywhere}.$$
--
--   Lévy's upward theorem (1937) is one of the basic martingale convergence theorems: conditional expectations along an increasing flow of information converge to the conditional expectation given all of it. It implies the Lévy zero–one law and is used throughout probability, ergodic theory and statistics.
--
--   **Formalization note.** Mathlib's `MeasureTheory.tendsto_ae_condExp`. `μ[g | m]` is the conditional expectation, and `⨆ n, ℱ n` is the σ-algebra generated by the filtration. Mathlib defines the conditional expectation of a non-integrable function to be $0$, so the statement holds trivially for such $g$ and needs no integrability hypothesis.
-- source:
--   Listed in Mathlib's curated theorem manifests (docs/1000.yaml); formalized in Mathlib as `MeasureTheory.tendsto_ae_condExp`. Proof here reduces to that Mathlib result.

import Mathlib

namespace FamousTheorems

open MeasureTheory

theorem levy_upward_theorem {Ω : Type*} {m0 : MeasurableSpace Ω} {μ : Measure Ω} {ℱ : Filtration ℕ m0} [IsFiniteMeasure μ]
    (g : Ω → ℝ) :
    ∀ᵐ x ∂μ, Filter.Tendsto (fun n => μ[g | ℱ n] x) Filter.atTop (nhds (μ[g | ⨆ n, ℱ n] x)) := by sorry

end FamousTheorems
